read $0.3594$ bits against a permutation null of $0.3591$ ($z = +0.11$): no signal at all,
unchanged from four dials to six.

\subsection{Contributions}

We convert those patterns into theorems and, in one case, into a counterexample: a finitary
counting calculus (\S\ref{sec:calc}); the ceiling theorem and its slack identity
(\S\ref{sec:ceiling}); the saturation theorem with a quantitative $\log m$ bound
(\S\ref{sec:sat}); strong subadditivity of the counting entropy (\S\ref{sec:ssa}) and
monotonicity of the curve (\S\ref{sec:mono}); the synergy law, the guarded monotone-deficit
law, strictness at a completing dial and an explicit counterexample to the unguarded law
(\S\ref{sec:syn}); and an exact vanishing theorem for symmetric batteries (\S\ref{sec:blind}).

\section{The counting calculus}\label{sec:calc}

Throughout, $\Omega$ is a finite non-empty \textbf{population} of size $N = |\Omega|$, and a
\textbf{statistic} is a function $f : \Omega \to A$ into an arbitrary set of readings. No
probability measure is assumed: all quantities are functions of cell counts.

\begin{definition}[Fibre, image, count]
For a statistic $f$ and a value $a$, the \emph{fibre} is $f^{-1}(a)$, the \emph{count} is
$n_f(a) = |f^{-1}(a)|$, and $\img f$ is the set of values attained.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Counting entropy]
With $\varphi(N,n) = -\frac nN \log \frac nN$ for $n \ge 1$ and $\varphi(N,0)=0$,
\[
H(f) \;=\; \sum_{a \in \img f} \varphi\big(N, n_f(a)\big)
\;=\; -\sum_{a \in \img f} \frac{n_f(a)}{N}\log\frac{n_f(a)}{N} \quad \text{(nats)},
\]
and $H_2(f) = H(f)/\log 2$ in bits.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Joint statistic, mutual information]
For $f : \Omega \to A$ and $g : \Omega \to B$, the \emph{joint} statistic is
$(f,g) : x \mapsto (f(x),g(x))$, and
\[
I(f;g) \;=\; H(f) + H(g) - H(f,g).
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Coarsening]\label{lem:coarse}
If $f = r \circ g$ then $H(f) \le H(g)$, with equality when $r$ is injective on $\img g$, and
strictly when $r$ identifies two values that $g$ attains on individuals it separates.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Each cell of $f$ is a disjoint union of cells of $g$, and
$\varphi(N, m_1+m_2) \le \varphi(N,m_1) + \varphi(N,m_2)$, with equality only if one of the
$m_i$ vanishes.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Subadditivity]\label{lem:sub}
$H(f,g) \le H(f) + H(g)$; hence $I(f;g) \ge 0$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Gibbs' inequality against the product reference $q(a,b) = \frac{n_f(a)}{N}\cdot\frac{n_g(b)}{N}$,
a probability weight on pairs.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Projections and swap]\label{lem:proj}
$H(f) \le H(f,g)$ and $H(g,f) = H(f,g)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Constants and non-constants]\label{lem:const}
A constant statistic has $H = 0$; if $f(x) \neq f(y)$ for some $x,y$ then $H(f) > 0$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{definition}[Dial, battery, joint reading]
A \textbf{dial} is a statistic $k : \Omega \to \{0,\dots,m-1\}$. A \textbf{battery} is a family
$d = (d_i)_{i\in\iota}$ of dials over a finite index set. For $S \subseteq \iota$ the
\textbf{joint reading} is $K_S(x) = (d_i(x))_{i \in S}$. For $S \subseteq T$ one has
$K_S = \rho_{T\to S}\circ K_T$, restriction of coordinates.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Capacity curve, deficit]
For a label $L : \Omega \to \Lambda$,
\[
\cpc(S) = I(K_S;L), \qquad D(S) = \cpc(S) - \sum_{i\in S} I(d_i;L).
\]
Along a nested chain we write $I(k) = \cpc(S_k)$, $D(k) = D(S_k)$.
\end{definition}

\section{The ceiling}\label{sec:ceiling}

\begin{theorem}[Ceiling]\label{thm:ceiling}
For every reading $K$ and label $L$, $I(K;L) \le H(L)$. In particular $\cpc(S) \le H(L)$ for
every sub-battery, of any size.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:proj}, $H(K) \le H(K,L)$; substitute into $I(K;L) = H(K)+H(L)-H(K,L)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Exact slack]\label{prop:slack}
$H(L) - I(K;L) = H(K,L) - H(K) = H(L\mid K) = \sum_c \frac{n_K(c)}{N}\,H\big(L\restriction K^{-1}(c)\big)$.
\end{proposition}

Thus the ceiling gap is exactly the residual within-cell label ambiguity: a percentage of
ceiling is a statement about how many distinct labels survive inside a typical cell.

\section{Saturation}\label{sec:sat}

\begin{theorem}[Saturation]\label{thm:sat}
$I(K;L) = H(L)$ if and only if $K$ determines $L$, i.e.\ $K(x) = K(y) \Rightarrow L(x) = L(y)$
for all $x,y \in \Omega$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
($\Leftarrow$) Determination gives a factorisation $L = r \circ K$, so $(K,L)$ has the same
cells as $K$ and $H(K,L) = H(K)$, whence $I(K;L) = H(L)$.
($\Rightarrow$) If $K(x) = K(y)$ but $L(x) \ne L(y)$, then $(K,L)$ separates $x,y$ while its
coarsening $K$ does not, so $H(K) < H(K,L)$ by the strict case of Lemma~\ref{lem:coarse}, and
$I(K;L) < H(L)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Strict sub-saturation]\label{cor:strict}
If a battery confuses two individuals with distinct labels, $\cpc(S) < H(L)$ strictly.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Quantitative saturation]\label{thm:quant}
Suppose $g : \Omega \to \{1,\dots,m\}$ is such that $L$ is a function of $(K,g)$. Then
\[
H(L) - I(K;L) \;\le\; \log m .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$(K,L)$ is a coarsening of $(K,g)$, so $H(K,L) \le H(K,g) \le H(K) + H(g)$ by
Lemmas~\ref{lem:coarse} and~\ref{lem:sub}, and $H(g) \le \log|\img g| \le \log m$. Conclude with
Proposition~\ref{prop:slack}.
\end{proof}

With $m=1$ this recovers exact saturation. It is the tool that converts ``$99.6\%$ of ceiling''
into a statement about residual ambiguity.

\section{Strong subadditivity of the counting entropy}\label{sec:ssa}

\begin{theorem}[Strong subadditivity]\label{thm:ssa}
For any statistics $u,v,w$ on a finite population,
\[
H(u,v,w) + H(u) \;\le\; H(u,v) + H(u,w).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Write $n(a), n(a,b), n(a,c), n(a,b,c)$ for the cell counts of $u$, $(u,v)$, $(u,w)$, $(u,v,w)$
and set
\[
p(a,b,c) = \frac{n(a,b,c)}{N}, \qquad
q(a,b,c) = \frac{n(a,b)\,n(a,c)}{n(a)\,N}.
\]
\emph{Step 1.} Fibre-wise partition gives $\sum_c n(a,b,c) = n(a,b)$, $\sum_b n(a,b,c) = n(a,c)$
and $\sum_{b,c} n(a,b,c) = n(a)$. Hence for fixed $a$ with $n(a) > 0$,
$\sum_{b,c} q(a,b,c) = n(a)^2/(n(a)N) = n(a)/N$, and summing over $a$ gives $\sum q = 1$;
likewise $\sum p = 1$.

\emph{Step 2.} For $p \ge 0$, $q > 0$, the inequality $\log t \le t-1$ at $t = q/p$ yields the
Gibbs bound $p\log\frac1p \le p\log\frac1q + (q-p)$ (the left side vanishing at $p=0$). Apply it
to every triple $(a,b,c) \in \img u \times \img v \times \img w$ and expand
$\log(1/q) = \log\frac{N}{n(a,b)} + \log\frac{N}{n(a,c)} - \log\frac{N}{n(a)}$.

\emph{Step 3.} Summing, the left side gives $H(u,v,w)$; the marginalisation identities collapse
the three log-sums to $H(u,v)$, $H(u,w)$ and $-H(u)$; and the correction terms sum to
$\sum q - \sum p = 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Conditioning reduces entropy]
$H(w \mid u,v) \le H(w\mid u)$, i.e.\ $H(u,v,w) - H(u,v) \le H(u,w) - H(u)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{remark}
The reference $q$ is the unique weight matching the $(u,v)$ and $(u,w)$ marginals of $p$ under
which $v$ and $w$ are conditionally independent given $u$; the defect in
Theorem~\ref{thm:ssa} is the conditional mutual information $I(v;w\mid u) = \mathrm{KL}(p\Vert q)$.
\end{remark}

\section{Monotonicity of the capacity curve}\label{sec:mono}

\begin{theorem}[Data processing for mutual information]\label{thm:dpi}
If $K = r \circ K'$ then $I(K;L) \le I(K';L)$ for every label $L$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:ssa} with $u = K$, $v = K'$, $w = L$ gives
$H(K,K',L) + H(K) \le H(K,K') + H(K,L)$. Since $K$ is a function of $K'$, $H(K,K') = H(K')$ and
$H(K,K',L) = H(K',L)$. Rearranging yields $I(K;L) \le I(K';L)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[The curve never decreases]\label{cor:mono}
$S \subseteq T \Rightarrow \cpc(S) \le \cpc(T)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Invariance under re-coding]\label{cor:recode}
Readings that determine each other have the same capacity for every label; capacity depends only
on the induced partition of the population, so the CRT identification of a tuple of coprime
residues with a single residue changes nothing.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proposition}[Marginals reproduce their dials]\label{prop:marg}
$\cpc(\{i\}) = I(d_i;L)$.
\end{proposition}

\section{Synergy, the deficit, and the boundary of the law}\label{sec:syn}

\begin{definition}[Independent readings]
$k_1, k_2$ are \emph{independent in the population} if $H(k_1,k_2) = H(k_1) + H(k_2)$;
equivalently $I(k_1;k_2) = 0$; equivalently $n(a,b)\,N = n_{k_1}(a)\,n_{k_2}(b)$.
\end{definition}

For a population uniformly distributed over a CRT modulus $\prod_i m_i$ with pairwise coprime
$m_i$, the residue readings are independent.

\begin{theorem}[Synergy law]\label{thm:syn}
If $k_1$ and $k_2$ are independent in the population, then for every label $L$
\[
I(k_1;L) + I(k_2;L) \;\le\; I\big((k_1,k_2);L\big).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:ssa} with $u = L$, $v = k_1$, $w = k_2$, together with invariance of entropy
under reordering of coordinates, gives $H(k_1,k_2,L) + H(L) \le H(k_1,L) + H(k_2,L)$. Then
\[
I((k_1,k_2);L) = H(k_1,k_2) + H(L) - H(k_1,k_2,L) \ge H(k_1) + H(k_2) + 2H(L) - H(k_1,L) - H(k_2,L),
\]
using independence, and the right-hand side is $I(k_1;L) + I(k_2;L)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Monotone deficit, guarded]\label{thm:defmono}
If $j \notin S$ and the dial $d_j$ is independent in the population of $K_S$, then
$D(S) \le D(S \cup \{j\})$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:syn} to $k_1 = K_S$, $k_2 = d_j$; the reading $(K_S,d_j)$ and
$K_{S\cup\{j\}}$ determine each other, so Corollary~\ref{cor:recode} identifies the right-hand
side with $\cpc(S\cup\{j\})$. Subtract the additive column, which grows by exactly $I(d_j;L)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Strict growth at the completing dial]\label{thm:complete}
Let $j \notin S$ with $I(d_j;L) = 0$. If $K_{S\cup\{j\}}$ determines $L$ while $K_S$ confuses
two individuals with distinct labels, then $D(S) < D(S \cup \{j\})$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$\cpc(S\cup\{j\}) = H(L)$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:sat} and $\cpc(S) < H(L)$ by
Corollary~\ref{cor:strict}; the additive columns agree since $I(d_j;L)=0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Negative deficit from duplication]\label{thm:dup}
If $i \ne j$, $d_i = d_j$ and $I(d_i;L) > 0$, then $D(\{i,j\}) = -I(d_i;L) < 0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$K_{\{i,j\}}$ and $d_i$ determine each other, so $\cpc(\{i,j\}) = I(d_i;L)$, while the additive
column is $2I(d_i;L)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{example}[Explicit counterexample]\label{ex:dup}
Let $\Omega = \{\mathrm{false},\mathrm{true}\}$ with the identity label and a battery of two
copies of the one-bit dial $k(x) = [\,x=\mathrm{true}\,]$. Then $\cpc = 1$ bit while the
additive bookkeeping reports $2$ bits: the deficit is $-1$ bit. Hence the unguarded
monotone-deficit claim is false, and the independence hypothesis of
Theorem~\ref{thm:defmono} cannot be dropped.
\end{example}

\begin{proposition}[Deficit cap]\label{prop:defcap}
$D(S) \le H(L) - \sum_{i\in S} I(d_i;L)$: compounding synergy is squeezed out by the ceiling.
\end{proposition}

\section{The exact vanishing theorem: symmetric batteries are blind}\label{sec:blind}

Let $W : \Omega \to \{\mathrm{false},\mathrm{true}\}$ be a binary label and $\sigma$ an
involution of $\Omega$.

\begin{lemma}[Cell-halving identity]\label{lem:half}
$2\varphi(N,m) = \varphi(N,2m) + \frac{2m}{N}\log 2$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Balanced halves]\label{lem:bal}
If $C(\sigma x) = C(x)$ and $W(\sigma x) = \lnot W(x)$ for all $x$, then for every value $c$,
$n_{(C,W)}(c,\mathrm{true}) = n_{(C,W)}(c,\mathrm{false})$, and the two counts sum to $n_C(c)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
$\sigma$ is a self-inverse bijection between the two halves of the cell.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[One extra bit]\label{thm:onebit}
Under the hypotheses of Lemma~\ref{lem:bal}, $H(C,W) = H(C) + \log 2$ and $H(W) = \log 2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Every cell of $C$, of size $2m_c$, splits into equal halves, contributing
$2\varphi(N,m_c) = \varphi(N,2m_c) + \frac{2m_c}{N}\log 2$ to $H(C,W)$ by
Lemma~\ref{lem:half}; sum over $c$ and use $\sum_c n_C(c)/N = 1$. Taking $C$ constant gives
$H(W) = \log 2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Factor blindness]\label{thm:blind}
If an involution preserves the reading $C$ and flips the binary label $W$, then $I(C;W) = 0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$I(C;W) = H(C) + \log 2 - (H(C) + \log 2) = 0$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:onebit}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[The wall at every battery size]\label{cor:wall}
If an involution preserves every dial of a battery and flips $W$, then $\cpc(S) = 0$ for every
sub-battery $S$. Monotonicity cannot help: a non-decreasing curve pinned at $0$ stays at $0$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{example}[A non-vacuous witness]\label{ex:swap}
Let $\Omega = \{(a,b)\in(\mathbb{Z}/5)^2 : a \ne b\}$, a population of $20$ ordered pairs of
distinct residues --- a toy model of semiprimes with their two factors. Let the dial read the
symmetric function $k(a,b) = (a+b)\bmod 5$ and the label be the which-factor bit
$W(a,b) = [\,a<b\,]$. The swap $\sigma(a,b) = (b,a)$ preserves the dial and flips the bit, so
$\cpc = 0$ exactly, even though $H(k) > 0$ and $H(W) = \log 2$.
\end{example}

Consequently any battery built from symmetric functions of a pair is structurally blind to the
order of the pair, at any size. The measured $0.3594$ bits against a permutation null of
$0.3591$ ($z=+0.11$) is finite-sample sparse-table bias around a structural zero.

\section{Consistency with the measured curve}

The theorems constrain Table~\ref{tab:curve} in four independent ways, all satisfied:
monotonicity of the curve (Corollary~\ref{cor:mono}); strict sub-ceiling behaviour
(Theorem~\ref{thm:ceiling}); monotonicity of the deficit (Theorem~\ref{thm:defmono}, whose
hypothesis the pairwise coprime conductors deliver); and, by Corollary~\ref{cor:strict}, the
structural diagnosis that even at $99.6\%$ the six-dial battery still confuses at least two
differently labelled individuals.

The gap $12.7726 - 12.7235 = 0.0491$ bits is, by Proposition~\ref{prop:slack}, exactly the
population-averaged within-cell label entropy: if the residual ambiguity is a balanced binary
choice concentrated on a fraction $\alpha$ of the population, then $\alpha \approx 4.9\%$.
The factor $3.7$ between $I(6) = 12.7235$ and the additive $5.3650$ is Theorem~\ref{thm:syn}
iterated six times; by Proposition~\ref{prop:defcap} the deficit is capped by
$12.7726 - 5.3650 = 7.4076$ bits against a measured $7.3585$, i.e.\ $99.3\%$ of its own
structural maximum.

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{Counting entropy by chained labelling.} A joint reading of $k$ dials is encoded as an
integer key by Horner's rule, $c \leftarrow c\,m_i + k_i$; the $N$ keys are then sorted or hashed
and run lengths counted. Cost $O(kN + N\log N)$ with $O(N)$ memory independent of the moduli ---
a dense table over the six-dial CRT modulus chained with a pair label would need of order
$10^{12}$ cells.

\paragraph{Capacity curve and deficit.} Compute $H(L)$ once; for each $k$ compute $H(K_k)$ and
$H(K_k,L)$, then $I(k) = H(K_k)+H(L)-H(K_k,L)$ and $D(k) = I(k) - \sum_{i\le k} I(d_i;L)$. All
four laws are checkable online: monotonicity, the ceiling, the sign of $D$, and independence
$H(K_k,d_{k+1}) = H(K_k) + H(d_{k+1})$ of the next dial. Total cost $O(nN\log N)$.

\paragraph{Saturation certificate and ambiguity audit.} Group the population by joint reading and
count distinct labels per cell. Pure cells everywhere certify exact saturation
(Theorem~\ref{thm:sat}); otherwise report the exact gap $H(K,L)-H(K)$, the fraction of the
population in impure cells, and $\log\max_c(\text{distinct labels in } c)$, a cell-local upper
bound for the gap. Cost $O(N\log N)$.

\section{Discussion}

Every object here is a partition of a finite set and every theorem an inequality between finite
sums of $\varphi(N,n)$. Two consequences matter. First, hypotheses are checkable by inspection
of the data rather than assumed of a generative model: ``independent in the population'' is the
finite identity $n(a,b)N = n(a)n(b)$. Second, exact vanishing results such as
Theorem~\ref{thm:blind} become possible, since the involution argument is a bijection of finite
sets --- which is what distinguishes a structural wall from a weak signal.

For practice, Theorem~\ref{thm:complete} warns that an instrument with exactly zero marginal
information can complete a classifier, so marginal ranking is unsafe; Theorem~\ref{thm:dup}
warns that additive bookkeeping over-credits redundant instruments. The correct diagnostic is
the deficit together with an independence audit. Corollary~\ref{cor:wall} is a no-go theorem:
questions distinguishing configurations related by a symmetry of the instruments are
unanswerable by those instruments at any scale.

The quantities computed here are plug-in statistics of the observed population and are biased
upward on sparse tables --- exactly what a permutation null detects. The synergy law assumes
exact independence; a robust version with $I(k_1;k_2) \le \varepsilon$ should hold with an
additive $\varepsilon$ slack, obtained by tracking the defect in Theorem~\ref{thm:syn}.

\section{Future directions}

\paragraph{Sharp per-cell saturation gap.} Theorem~\ref{thm:quant} uses a global disambiguator;
the sharp statement should be local: the ceiling gap is at most the logarithm of the largest
number of distinct labels inside a single cell, and equals the weighted average of per-cell
label entropies. The Gibbs argument of Theorem~\ref{thm:ssa} works cell by cell with uniform
reference $1/m_c$, so per-cell multiplicities control the gap, turning a measured percentage
into a bound on residual per-cell ambiguity.

\paragraph{A robust synergy law.} Replace exact independence with $I(k_1;k_2)\le\varepsilon$ and
track the accumulation of defects along a chain of $n$ nearly independent dials.

\paragraph{Optimal dial ordering.} Which nested chain maximises the curve at each $k$? Capacity
is not submodular --- the synergy law is a supermodular-type inequality --- so the guarantees of
greedy selection are open.

\paragraph{Finite-sample corrections.} A bias-corrected plug-in estimator would replace the
permutation-null comparison and attach a confidence interval to ``percentage of ceiling''.

\paragraph{Symmetry detection.} Can one search efficiently for an involution preserving a battery
and flipping a label, turning Corollary~\ref{cor:wall} into an automatic pre-flight check?

\section{Conclusion}

A growing battery obeys a capacity law with three exact components: it cannot exceed the label
entropy, and equals it precisely when it classifies perfectly; it never decreases as instruments
are added, by strong subadditivity of the counting entropy; and it exceeds the sum of its parts
exactly when the instruments are independent in the population, the inequality reversing under
duplication. When the instruments share a symmetry the label breaks, capacity is identically
zero at every size. A six-dial curve rising to $99.6\%$ of its ceiling with a monotonically
compounding deficit and a flat which-factor reading is, in every detail, the behaviour these
theorems require.

